It seems so :-).  I never said that you should use a thorough test of 
*one* condition as the basis of a conclusion about the other.  I said 
you can try to generalize but that it is less justified than drawing 
conclusions about the same situation appearing again.  Generalization to 
a different situation would be based on assuming that the two situations 
belong to the same *class* of (types of) events.  Generalization or 
reapplication to an event that looks the same for all practical purposes 
to the player would start from the assumption that they *are* the same 
type of event.  Both kinds of generalization are possible and legitimate 
but the latter is more failsafe, so to say.  And if you want to base any 
conclusions on a *thourough* test, you should do that on the basis of 
the second approach, not the first.

In the case of Ellora's story, one might of course debate whether when 
she filled up a part of Medusa's moat with ten boulders she was working 
on a small or on a big moat.  I read the story so that she was using the 
ten boulders to completely fill a part of the moat that was separated 
from the rest by land tongues and maybe had only one exit to the big 
moat.  Then she would have, in both preceding experiences, filled rather 
small moats (the two pools in the swamp and this ten square moat on 
Medusa's level).  But pushing the last boulder that sank, she was 
entering the range of the large sea.  Of course, I am not discussing the 
mechanism here how things are implemented in the game.  The thing I 
wanted to point out was that there possibly was a difference of the last 
situation in comparison with the two preceding ones that might have made 
her wary.  Since I did not have the map of Medusa's level in my mind, I 
may simply have been wrong in this assumption (but any experienced 
player will realize that it was a reasonable assumption and why).  Then 
by the second filling of a moat, where she used ten boulders, she was 
already working on the big sea, and it would indeed have been pretty 
difficult for her to be wary ahead of time.
